<h2 id=3D"intuition---recursive">Intuition - Recursive</h2>
<p>Sometimes, the interviewer may want the problem solved using recursion. =
Let=E2=80=99s see what a recursive solution to this problem would look like=
.</p>
<p>In a recursive solution, the problem is solved by <strong>solving smalle=
r instances of the same problem</strong>. To solve these smaller subproblem=
s, we would need to use the linked list reversal function (<code>linked_lis=
t_reversal</code>) in its own implementation. The process of solving smalle=
r problems using recursive calls continues until the smallest version of th=
e problem is solved.</p>
<p>The smallest version of this problem involves reversing a linked list of=
 size 0 or 1. These are linked lists which are inherently the same as their=
 reverse. So, these can be our <strong>base cases</strong>.</p>
<p>With this in mind, let's try crafting the logic of the recursive functio=
n using the example below:</p>
